A||||4||Taijiguy||Flywheel identification ?||||1||
Z||000000||Taijiguy||04-12-2001||10:15 AM||||*sigh* Have to put a new flywheel on my 85GT. I know this is externally balanced. How do I verify the newly acquired flywheel is the right one, and not for an internally balanced motor? (is this a dumb question?)<BR>~Taiji||||reg||1
Z||000001||Steve Normington||04-12-2001||10:38 AM||||A flywheel for an externally balanced engine will have a big honkin' chuck of metal welded on the back side (the side towards the engine).<P>PS. There is no such thing as a dumb question when GM is involved.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>85 SE 2M6||||reg||1
Z||000002||Taijiguy||04-12-2001||12:54 PM||||OK, cool. I thought that was the case, but wasn't positive. Something so obvious...well, that's why I wondered if it were a dumb-ass question LOL<BR>Thanks alot<BR>~Taiji||||reg||1
Z||000003||Greg Piet||05-02-2001||09:48 PM||||To add to the stupid questions...<BR>There isnt a ring gear on the flywheels for a muncie 4 speed is there????<BR>(there is no center gear thingy in the center of my flywheel)||||reg||1
Z||000004||85Josh||05-03-2001||12:03 AM||||Greg, the ring gear on a flywheel is on the outside. It is what the starter engages to turn the motor over. Steve, if you think GM is bad, check out Fords sometime!<P>------------------<BR>85 2M4(Lil' Bit)||||reg||6
